\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Takeaway: one explanation, three predictions, three confirmations, and a constructive remainder that is already free.} The explanation is that a matching score is dominated by how predictable the reference text is, so it measures the answer key rather than the student. Correlations are against blind human soundness on the anchor set unless a ranking is named; intervals, subset sizes, and per-row caveats are in the tables of the sections named. Six directional predictions were registered in advance and four landed as called; the two misses, a sign flip and a falsification, are reported inline where they bite (Sections~\ref {sec:repairs} and~\ref {sec:verify}).}}{2}{table.caption.1}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{tab:predictions}{{1}{2}{\textbf {Takeaway: one explanation, three predictions, three confirmations, and a constructive remainder that is already free.} The explanation is that a matching score is dominated by how predictable the reference text is, so it measures the answer key rather than the student. Correlations are against blind human soundness on the anchor set unless a ranking is named; intervals, subset sizes, and per-row caveats are in the tables of the sections named. Six directional predictions were registered in advance and four landed as called; the two misses, a sign flip and a falsification, are reported inline where they bite (Sections~\ref {sec:repairs} and~\ref {sec:verify})}{table.caption.1}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{One alternative explanation.}{2}{section*.3}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Takeaway: every matching metric straddles zero or sits weakly negative against blind human soundness, while the verification signals carry real signal, and the top of the field is the bit everyone already has for free at $+0.92$.} Pearson correlation with blind human soundness on the 60-item anchor set, with bootstrap 95\% CIs: matching metrics in orange, verification signals in blue, LLM judges (expensive comparison points) as hollow markers. Unigram-F1's CI excludes zero on the negative side, uncorrected for the five-metric battery, so that single exclusion is suggestive rather than confirmatory. Dotted lines mark the inter-rater agreement and the implied single-rater ceiling; both reliability estimates have wide intervals, so ceiling comparisons are indicative only.}}{3}{figure.caption.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:class}{{1}{3}{\textbf {Takeaway: every matching metric straddles zero or sits weakly negative against blind human soundness, while the verification signals carry real signal, and the top of the field is the bit everyone already has for free at $+0.92$.} Pearson correlation with blind human soundness on the 60-item anchor set, with bootstrap 95\% CIs: matching metrics in orange, verification signals in blue, LLM judges (expensive comparison points) as hollow markers. Unigram-F1's CI excludes zero on the negative side, uncorrected for the five-metric battery, so that single exclusion is suggestive rather than confirmatory. Dotted lines mark the inter-rater agreement and the implied single-rater ceiling; both reliability estimates have wide intervals, so ceiling comparisons are indicative only}{figure.caption.2}{}}
